# Documentation

- **Primary principle**: Code and comments are the primary documentation. Create MD files only when they add lasting value.
- **When to create docs**: Create documentation files for:
  - Architecture decisions and system design
  - Data lineage and workflow documentation (required)
  - API references and usage guides
  - Onboarding and setup instructions
  - Long-term reference material that won't become stale quickly
- **When to avoid docs**: Don't create MD files for:
  - Progress updates or session notes (use chat/commits instead)
  - Temporary analysis or one-off experiments
  - Information that changes frequently and should live in code
- **Maintenance**: If documentation exists, keep it current:
  - Review and update docs when making related code changes
  - Archive or remove outdated documentation promptly
  - Prefer updating existing docs over creating new ones
- **Code documentation**: All scripts should include:
  - Purpose and usage in docstring
  - Required arguments and options
  - Example usage
  - Dependencies (data lineage orders, environment variables)
  - Error conditions and exit codes
- **Lineage documentation**: All data processing scripts MUST include lineage comments (see Data Lineage Architecture section)
- **Code comments**: Use comments to explain "why", not "what"
  - Document non-obvious decisions, performance optimizations, and workarounds
  - Include references to issues, PRs, or discussions when relevant
  - Keep comments up-to-date with code changes
